1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,035
Where does a payment system start, if not with payments?

2
00:00:03,035 --> 00:00:07,666
It starts with a project that builds, and an environment that runs with one command.

3
00:00:07,666 --> 00:00:13,976
We use Java twenty-five and Spring Boot four, built by one Gradle build with a module for each part of the domain.

4
00:00:13,976 --> 00:00:23,845
The app has five modules, partner, commission, terminal, card, and payment, and each one owns its own schema, with a database user that can see nothing else.

5
00:00:23,845 --> 00:00:30,071
A boundary test fails the build if one module reaches into another module's code or tables.

6
00:00:30,071 --> 00:00:36,369
Shared pieces live in a platform folder, built once and published to the local Maven repository.

7
00:00:36,369 --> 00:00:42,095
The version catalog pins every version, so the build is the same on every machine.

8
00:00:42,095 --> 00:00:48,047
The error starter gives every module the same error format: a problem detail with a stable code.

9
00:00:48,047 --> 00:00:56,928
API contracts are OpenAPI files, and the code generated from them lives only in the published jars, never in our source.

10
00:00:56,928 --> 00:01:05,571
Every module follows the same layers, controller, service, repository, and entity, with its constants in one place.

11
00:01:05,571 --> 00:01:13,476
A separate infrastructure repository uses Docker Compose to start Postgres, Redis, and Keycloak, and nothing else yet.

12
00:01:13,476 --> 00:01:16,619
Every other tool joins only when a problem needs it.

13
00:01:16,619 --> 00:01:24,297
With one setting, the app logs structured JSON in the community ECS format, and reports its health from the very first minute.

14
00:01:24,297 --> 00:01:28,821
One command in the infrastructure repository, and the whole world is up.

15
00:01:28,821 --> 00:01:37,226
Unit tests run in seconds, integration tests run against a real Postgres, and load tests live in their own k6 repository.

16
00:01:37,226 --> 00:01:40,690
Next, we design the domain before we write a single table.

